<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Itakh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/849">849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Aytakh al-Khazari, Itakh al-Khazari, al-Tabbakh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Itakh al-Khazari was a leading commander of the Turkic army of the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> said to have worked as a slave cook before al-Mu'tasim bought him as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulam</a> in 815. He rose to command the caliph's personal guard, took part in the Sack of Amorium and under al-Wathiq was named governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In 847 he helped choose al-Mutawakkil as caliph and on his orders arrested the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Ibn al-Zayyat, who was tortured to death, becoming chamberlain and head of the barid, the post and intelligence service. Sent on pilgrimage in 848, he was arrested on his return and died of thirst in prison in 849.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Itakh's nisba, al-Khazari, marks him as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> by origin. He is said to have worked as a slave in the kitchen of Sallam al-Abrash al-Khadim, which earned him the nickname al-Tabbakh, the cook, before he was bought as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghulam">ghulam</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a>, who reigned as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> from 833 to 842. He rose to become one of the senior commanders of al-Mu'tasim's Turkic guard and took part in several expeditions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a> he served as sahib al-shurta at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> and commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s personal guard. By the accession of al-Wathiq in 842 he and the Turk Ashinas were the mainstay of the caliphate. Al-Wathiq made him governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> in 843/4, and after the death of Ashinas in 844/5 named him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, though Itakh appointed Harthamah ibn al-Nadr al-Jabali to govern there in his place. Ya'qubi reports that he also received the governorships of Khurasan, al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a> and the districts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>.</p><p>When al-Wathiq died unexpectedly in August 847, Itakh met with the vizier Muhammad ibn al-Zayyat, the chief qadi Ahmad ibn Abi Duwad and the Turkish general Wasif al-Turki to choose a successor. Ibn al-Zayyat proposed al-Wathiq's son Muhammad, the future al-Muhtadi, but he was passed over for his youth in favour of another son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim">al-Mu'tasim</a>, the 26-year-old Ja'far, who became al-Mutawakkil. The new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> was resolved to destroy the circle of his father's officials, and his first target was Ibn al-Zayyat, who had once treated him with disrespect.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 22 September 847 al-Mutawakkil sent Itakh to summon the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> as if to an audience; instead Ibn al-Zayyat was taken to Itakh's residence and held there. This was the height of Itakh's career, when he combined the posts of chamberlain, head of the personal guard, intendant of the palace and head of the barid, the public post that also served as the government's intelligence network.</p><p>In 848 he was persuaded to go on the pilgrimage and laid down his powers, only to be arrested on his return. His possessions were confiscated, and in his house alone the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s agents reportedly found one million gold dinars. He died of thirst in prison the following year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Itakh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Turkish commanders such as Ashinas, <b>Itakh</b> and Bugha the Elder became the most powerful men in the state, and when pay failed their troops rioted: in 861–870 they murdered or deposed four caliphs.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
